1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:05,000
It's been ticking away since the deep chill of the Cold War.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:11,000
From the explosion of the Soviet's first atomic bomb, to today's new nuclear threats,

3
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:17,000
this icon of nuclear danger has gauged how close we have come to Armageddon.

4
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
The world will end at the stroke of midnight,

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:26,000
and only a few minutes remain on the Doomsday Clock, next on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:31,753
Good morning.

7
00:00:31,754 --> 00:00:41,600
I'm Stephen Schwartz, publisher of the Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists.

8
00:00:42,000 --> 00:00:46,780
February 27, 2002, a news conference in Chicago.

9
00:00:47,660 --> 00:00:55,420
There, George Lopez, chairman of the board for a magazine called Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists, issued a warning.

10
00:00:55,421 --> 00:01:02,480
The September 11th attacks and the subsequent and probably unrelated use of the mail to deliver deadly anthrax spores

11
00:01:02,481 --> 00:01:08,320
breached previous boundaries for terrorist acts and should have been a full global wake-up call.

12
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:16,180
Moving the clock's hands at this time reflects our growing concern that the international community has simply hit the snooze button

13
00:01:16,181 --> 00:01:18,560
rather than respond to the full alarm.

14
00:01:18,900 --> 00:01:21,500
Time, he said, was running out.

15
00:01:21,501 --> 00:01:33,874
To make the point dramatically clear, Nobel Prize winning physicist Leon Letterman set the minute hand at 7 minutes to midnight on a symbolic timepiece dubbed the

16
00:01:33,875 --> 00:01:35,020
Doomsday Clock.

17
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:39,700
It ominously suggested the nuclear danger the world faced.

18
00:01:39,701 --> 00:01:53,701
anything inside 10 minutes is worthy of pretty serious concern we think about both the events

19
00:01:54,360 --> 00:01:59,060
that have happened which is normally what the clock responds to and we think about what's

20
00:01:59,061 --> 00:02:02,925
likely to happen in the future.

21
00:02:02,926 --> 00:02:10,280
Since 1947, the minute hand of the doomsday clock has moved

22
00:02:10,281 --> 00:02:16,560
forward or backward 17 times as events have heightened or lessened the possibility of a

23
00:02:16,561 --> 00:02:30,561
nuclear war. The Enola Gay arrives at 811 a.m. In 10 seconds, the world will shake.

24
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:46,400
In August 1945, the United States detonated its first nuclear weapons in Japan, ending

25
00:02:46,401 --> 00:02:47,476
World War II.

26
00:02:47,477 --> 00:02:53,520
The bomb's awesome power and the deaths of more than 100,000 people under

27
00:02:53,521 --> 00:02:59,248
the mushroom clouds in Hiroshima and Nagasaki greatly troubled some scientists.

28
00:02:59,249 --> 00:02:59,948
They were

29
00:02:59,950 --> 00:03:04,440
part of the Manhattan Project, the government's atomic bomb program.

30
00:03:04,441 --> 00:03:10,280
They were afraid that they had unleashed a genie that could not ever be bottled, but

31
00:03:10,281 --> 00:03:14,120
they were going to do their damnedest to try to bottle it up again.

32
00:03:14,121 --> 00:03:21,540
Immediately after the war, a group of former Manhattan Project chemists and physicists began meeting in Chicago.

33
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:27,740
Among them, John A. Simpson, inventor of a plutonium detection instrument.

34
00:03:28,440 --> 00:03:32,940
Harold C. Urey, a 1934 Nobel Prize winner in chemistry.

35
00:03:33,740 --> 00:03:38,500
Alexander Langsdorf, the inventor of a chamber for tracking cosmic rays.

36
00:03:38,501 --> 00:03:44,820
and Eugene Rabinowich, co-author of the June 1945 memorandum

37
00:03:44,821 --> 00:03:49,800
that had urged the Secretary of War not to use the bomb against Japan.

38
00:03:50,400 --> 00:03:54,600
They wanted to break down the walls of secrecy, involve the public,

39
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:59,920
and help them understand what this really meant for our future.

40
00:04:00,560 --> 00:04:03,520
And they were all motivated by that feeling, all of them.

41
00:04:04,460 --> 00:04:08,080
They call themselves the Atomic Scientists of Chicago.

42
00:04:08,500 --> 00:04:15,260
And they wanted to open the eyes of the public and policymakers about the significance of atomic power.

43
00:04:15,860 --> 00:04:23,480
So they published a newsletter called Bulletin of the Atomic Scientists of Chicago in December 1945.

44
00:04:24,540 --> 00:04:30,360
They founded it also out of their desire to ensure that nuclear weapons would never again be used

45
00:04:30,361 --> 00:04:35,840
and that mankind might perhaps learn to harness the potential benefits of atomic energy.

46
00:04:36,800 --> 00:04:43,860
44-year-old Eugene Rabinowich, a Russian immigrant, became the Bulletin's first editor-in-chief.

47
00:04:44,360 --> 00:04:47,400
It was a title he held for more than 20 years.

48
00:04:47,640 --> 00:04:54,280
He was a small man, a round man, a very humble one, if you will, and really brilliant.

49
00:04:54,620 --> 00:04:56,060
There's no question about that.

50
00:04:56,061 --> 00:05:09,180
And he was a man dedicated, really dedicated, to the belief that science was a positive force in the world and for the benefit of people.

51
00:05:09,181 --> 00:05:12,900
And that's really what kind of pushed him forward all the time.

52
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:20,020
Initially, the Bulletin was simply a six-page mimeograph journal.

53
00:05:20,021 --> 00:05:27,020
By 1947, circulation was up and a magazine replaced the newsletter format.

54
00:05:27,021 --> 00:05:38,338
And so the editors asked Martell, a local artist who was also married to one of the founders of the Bulletin, Alexander Langsdorf, to come up with something to put on

55
00:05:38,339 --> 00:05:39,039
the cover.

56
00:05:39,040 --> 00:05:45,657
cover. One of the founders, High Goldsmith, knew about me as an artist.

57
00:05:45,658 --> 00:05:47,900
He gave no instructions.

58
00:05:47,901 --> 00:05:54,071
All he said is it can't cost much. It never was any money.

59
00:05:54,072 --> 00:05:58,220
So I did several concepts and I thought

60
00:05:58,221 --> 00:05:59,122
about it.

61
00:05:59,123 --> 00:06:07,340
All the scientists felt an urgency to explain what had happened with the bomb. And

62
00:06:07,341 --> 00:06:15,880
And because of the extreme urgency, I remember the clock seemed to be important.

63
00:06:18,680 --> 00:06:24,460
Martil's plan was to repeat a clock design every month on a different background color.

64
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:30,940
In June 1947, the first bulletin clock cover appeared.

65
00:06:31,760 --> 00:06:36,740
It depicted the final quadrant of a clock face with a minute hand approaching midnight.

66
00:06:37,340 --> 00:06:41,840
The message was clear. The end of time was drawing close.

67
00:06:42,500 --> 00:06:48,880
While Martell intended the clock image as a whole to convey a psychological sense of imminent danger,

68
00:06:49,580 --> 00:06:54,520
placing the minute hand at seven minutes to midnight was simply a matter of aesthetics.

69
00:06:54,521 --> 00:07:00,180
I chose seven minutes because it seemed the right time on the page.

70
00:07:00,181 --> 00:07:07,520
It was the right design. It left room for the contents and it just suited my eye.

71
00:07:08,360 --> 00:07:15,680
The following month, Eugene Rabinowitz's essay to readers explained the symbolism of the clock of doom, as he called it.

72
00:07:16,380 --> 00:07:25,541
It represents the state of mind of those whose closeness to the development of atomic energy does not permit them to forget that their lives and those of their

73
00:07:25,542 --> 00:07:26,242
children,

74
00:07:26,243 --> 00:07:31,740
the security of their country and the survival of civilization all hang in the balance as long

75
00:07:31,741 --> 00:07:35,340
as the specter of atomic war has not been exercised.

76
00:07:40,780 --> 00:07:47,500
October 1949, President Harry Truman made an announcement that brought real meaning

77
00:07:47,501 --> 00:07:53,038
to the doomsday clock. We have been confronted with a new powerful imperialism.

78
00:07:53,039 --> 00:07:54,300
The United States,

79
00:07:54,301 --> 00:07:58,962
he said, had evidence of an atomic explosion in the Soviet Union.

80
00:07:58,963 --> 00:08:00,540
The bulletin's editors

81
00:08:00,541 --> 00:08:07,520
knew that the East-West nuclear arms race that they had foreseen was now underway.

82
00:08:07,521 --> 00:08:14,380
The scientists explained very calmly that once it's obvious to the world that such weapons

83
00:08:14,381 --> 00:08:20,340
can be built, it's only a matter of time before they will be built by any technically competent

84
00:08:20,341 --> 00:08:23,060
country with access to the right materials and the right knowledge.

85
00:08:24,260 --> 00:08:30,580
In response to the Soviet's atomic bomb, Rabinowitz wrote in the October 1949 issue,

86
00:08:31,100 --> 00:08:36,300
We do not advise Americans that doomsday is near and that they can expect atomic bombs

87
00:08:36,301 --> 00:08:41,440
to start falling on their heads a month or a year from now, but we think they have reason

88
00:08:41,441 --> 00:08:45,960
to be deeply alarmed and to be prepared for grave decisions.

89
00:08:45,961 --> 00:08:53,860
To dramatize the point, Rabinowitz reset the clock's minute hand on the bulletin's cover, three minutes to midnight.

90
00:08:54,580 --> 00:08:58,600
The clock unexpectedly took on new meaning for the magazine's editors.

91
00:08:58,920 --> 00:09:05,780
Suddenly they realized they had a very important tool here that could help educate people.

92
00:09:06,520 --> 00:09:14,160
And in the beginning, it was certainly seen as a way to tell quickly and symbolically a public out there

93
00:09:14,161 --> 00:09:16,800
that, gee, life is better or life is not so good.

94
00:09:16,940 --> 00:09:18,680
Things are changing. You better watch out.

95
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:22,640
But the clock's symbolism didn't fully capture the public's attention

96
00:09:22,641 --> 00:09:27,920
until the explosion of a new, more powerful weapon of mass destruction.

97
00:09:27,921 --> 00:09:31,600
Four, three, two, one.

98
00:09:35,280 --> 00:09:41,100
In its day, the Manhattan Project was the largest industrial and scientific effort in history.

99
00:09:41,101 --> 00:09:47,840
It cost more than $2 billion in 1945 and employed more than 175,000 people.

100
00:09:48,600 --> 00:09:51,000
The Doomsday Clock will return in a moment.

101
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:58,700
Russia becomes an atomic power. America's monopoly is ended.

102
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:05,895
The Soviets' detonation of the atom bomb in 1949 rekindled a secret high-level U.S.

103
00:10:05,896 --> 00:10:06,596
debate

104
00:10:06,597 --> 00:10:09,420
left simmering since the early days of the Manhattan Project.

105
00:10:09,421 --> 00:10:12,420
Should the United States build a super bomb?

106
00:10:12,421 --> 00:10:16,420
A hydrogen bomb far more powerful than the largest atom bomb?

107
00:10:19,420 --> 00:10:24,420
These were weapons of a completely different order of magnitude.

108
00:10:24,421 --> 00:10:30,420
Atomic bombs could destroy parts of cities, or if they were small, entire cities.

109
00:10:30,421 --> 00:10:32,420
Whereas hydrogen bombs could destroy countries.

110
00:10:32,421 --> 00:10:36,420
And of course there was no limit to the explosive power of a hydrogen bomb.

111
00:10:36,421 --> 00:10:39,720
It was a time where the world itself was very unsettled.

112
00:10:39,840 --> 00:10:43,020
Independence movements in Africa, in Latin America.

113
00:10:45,020 --> 00:10:50,220
And then, of course, the struggle between the communist world and the non-communist world.

114
00:10:50,680 --> 00:10:54,160
You add to this the destructive power of nuclear weapons,

115
00:10:55,080 --> 00:10:58,400
and it's like you're building a big bonfire in the middle of everything.

116
00:10:58,740 --> 00:11:00,080
And who's going to light the fuse?

117
00:11:00,081 --> 00:11:12,960
On October 31, 1952, the United States detonated its first super bomb, a thermonuclear device

118
00:11:12,961 --> 00:11:14,820
with a human name, Mike.

119
00:11:15,300 --> 00:11:20,600
But in fact, it was a people killer, with a yield nearly 1,000 times greater than the

120
00:11:20,601 --> 00:11:21,920
bomb dropped on Hiroshima.

121
00:11:21,921 --> 00:11:25,400
Three, two, one.

122
00:11:30,080 --> 00:11:37,080
All test labs seem to be swept clean.

123
00:11:37,081 --> 00:11:40,080
Ujilab is completely gone.

124
00:11:40,081 --> 00:11:46,080
The test site, an atoll called Alujilab in the Marshall Islands, disappeared,

125
00:11:46,081 --> 00:11:51,080
leaving only a crater 160 feet deep and more than a mile wide.

126
00:11:51,081 --> 00:11:59,080
Nine months later, in August 1953, the Russians exploded a less powerful but still awesome thermonuclear device.

127
00:11:59,081 --> 00:12:09,260
when word of the soviet test got out the september 1953 bulletin cover was remade at the last moment

128
00:12:09,261 --> 00:12:16,120
eugene rabinowich again decided to reset the doomsday clock this time to two minutes to midnight

129
00:12:16,121 --> 00:12:24,040
the closest for midnight that the clock has ever been was 1953 this is life and death stuff the

130
00:12:24,041 --> 00:12:28,980
power of both countries to utterly annihilate each other and take the rest of the world with them

131
00:12:28,981 --> 00:12:31,080
was quickly becoming a reality.

132
00:12:31,380 --> 00:12:37,720
The bulletin editors weren't saying we're going to have a nuclear war right tomorrow because of this,

133
00:12:38,160 --> 00:12:40,480
but they were saying we'd crossed a threshold.

134
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:46,240
We were truly participating in a nuclear arms race that didn't have any finish line.

135
00:12:47,420 --> 00:12:51,440
The latest bulletin cover garnered great attention for the Doomsday Clock,

136
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:55,400
transforming it into one of the best-known symbols of the nuclear age.

137
00:12:55,401 --> 00:13:00,260
In the beginning, it was kind of called the Chicago clock, you know, the Chicago Bulletin.

138
00:13:00,261 --> 00:13:04,260
No one thought of it as a national institution.

139
00:13:04,261 --> 00:13:11,260
And when things became very serious, like when the H-bomb came about,

140
00:13:11,261 --> 00:13:15,040
I think the clock suddenly emerged as a national institution.

141
00:13:15,041 --> 00:13:17,560
It's just something that people, I think, intuitively understand.

142
00:13:17,561 --> 00:13:22,560
We may not understand exactly the symbolism of seven minutes versus five or three,

143
00:13:22,561 --> 00:13:28,080
but you can see it marching further away from or closer to midnight and understand what that means.

144
00:13:29,400 --> 00:13:36,700
Rabinowitz did not move the clock's minute hand again until a new threat in another part of the world reached the flashpoint.

145
00:13:42,420 --> 00:13:49,700
On October 31st, 1956, Egypt was invaded by Israel, Great Britain and France.

146
00:13:49,701 --> 00:13:54,220
They wanted to oust Egyptian leader Colonel Gamal Abdel Nasser,

147
00:13:54,700 --> 00:13:59,200
who in July had nationalized the British and French-owned Suez Canal.

148
00:14:01,080 --> 00:14:03,520
President Dwight Eisenhower faced a dilemma.

149
00:14:03,880 --> 00:14:09,260
If the United States supported its three allies, Nasser might turn to the Soviet Union for help.

150
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:17,020
President Eisenhower backed the United Nations and forced the British and the French and Israelis to back off.

151
00:14:17,021 --> 00:14:27,340
and the bulletin editors and the board of directors were always very much in favor of multilateral solutions to conflicts.

152
00:14:27,500 --> 00:14:31,080
Persuade Israeli, British and French troops to lay down their arm.

153
00:14:31,081 --> 00:14:37,560
In response to Eisenhower's efforts, Rabinowich set the doomsday clock back to seven minutes before midnight.

154
00:14:38,100 --> 00:14:40,340
In January 1960, he wrote,

155
00:14:40,600 --> 00:14:45,700
A new cohesive force has entered the interplay of forces shaping the face of mankind,

156
00:14:45,701 --> 00:14:49,600
and it is making the future of man a little less foreboding.

157
00:14:54,600 --> 00:14:59,740
Then, in October 1962, a confrontation between Washington and Moscow

158
00:14:59,741 --> 00:15:05,380
over the presence of Soviet missiles in Cuba brought the world to the brink of nuclear war.

159
00:15:05,740 --> 00:15:11,560
It shall be the policy of this nation to regard any nuclear missile launched from Cuba

160
00:15:11,561 --> 00:15:18,160
against any nation in the Western Hemisphere as an attack by the Soviet Union on the United States,

161
00:15:18,940 --> 00:15:23,120
requiring a full retaliatory response upon the Soviet Union.

162
00:15:23,121 --> 00:15:31,500
On Saturday afternoon, October 27, 1962, at 4 p.m., I remember it was over yesterday,

163
00:15:32,080 --> 00:15:40,360
the Joint Chiefs of Staff recommended we launch the planned attack, or the contingency attack,

164
00:15:40,361 --> 00:15:42,300
against Cuba.

165
00:15:44,060 --> 00:15:46,040
The first day's air attack was planned at

166
00:15:46,041 --> 00:15:50,100
1080 sorties. That's larger than any single day's attack on Kosovo.

167
00:15:50,640 --> 00:15:53,960
We had 180,000 troops mobilized in southeast

168
00:15:53,961 --> 00:15:56,100
U.S. ports with the shipping to take them there.

169
00:15:57,060 --> 00:15:58,600
We came that close.

170
00:16:02,840 --> 00:16:05,960
When the Soviet leader found out about the eminent U.S.

171
00:16:06,040 --> 00:16:08,360
invasion, he agreed to remove the missiles.

172
00:16:08,361 --> 00:16:11,920
And Russia has decided to end its billion-dollar venture.

173
00:16:12,740 --> 00:16:14,100
The missiles go home.

174
00:16:14,820 --> 00:16:18,540
There were 170 nuclear warrants on the soil of Cuba.

175
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:23,260
If our invasion force had attempted to land on Cuba,

176
00:16:23,340 --> 00:16:25,300
there's absolutely no question in my mind

177
00:16:25,301 --> 00:16:29,340
about what the Soviets would have used the tactical nuclear weapons.

178
00:16:29,760 --> 00:16:36,060
The Cuban Missile Crisis strangely failed to produce so much as a tick on the doomsday clock.

179
00:16:36,220 --> 00:16:38,320
My sense is that it was over too quickly.

180
00:16:38,360 --> 00:16:42,840
Remember, it was just the 13 days of October, and the clock reacts to trends.

181
00:16:42,980 --> 00:16:46,580
There's the irony of looking at that and saying, yes, that was a large event.

182
00:16:47,300 --> 00:16:51,020
But I think it's been larger in history than it was at the time.

183
00:16:51,120 --> 00:16:54,180
I'm not sure very many people knew we were that close at the time.

184
00:16:57,380 --> 00:16:59,960
Having peered into the nuclear abyss,

185
00:16:59,961 --> 00:17:04,500
U.S. and Soviet leaders put more emphasis on diplomacy.

186
00:17:04,501 --> 00:17:13,380
Each side talked about a test ban treaty of some kind, hopefully one that would ban tests

187
00:17:13,381 --> 00:17:16,660
in the atmosphere and underground tests as well.

188
00:17:16,661 --> 00:17:24,000
But we were concerned that we would not be able to know when they were carrying out underground

189
00:17:24,001 --> 00:17:25,160
tests.

190
00:17:25,161 --> 00:17:32,660
So we gave up, sadly from my point of view, we gave up the negotiations on unlimited

191
00:17:32,661 --> 00:17:39,100
test ban and we we put through just barely by the way a limited test ban.

192
00:17:39,101 --> 00:17:43,220
The president heals the agreement with Russia with a note of caution.

193
00:17:43,221 --> 00:17:50,340
In 1963 the two countries signed the partial test ban treaty eliminating all but underground

194
00:17:50,341 --> 00:17:52,180
nuclear tests.

195
00:17:52,181 --> 00:17:57,040
The clock did react when the partial test ban treaty was signed and that was negotiated

196
00:17:57,041 --> 00:18:02,640
as a direct consequence of the enormous fear that everybody felt out of the Cuban

197
00:18:02,641 --> 00:18:03,477
missile crisis.

198
00:18:03,478 --> 00:18:07,720
So the clock did react, it just reacted later to an event that was connected

199
00:18:07,721 --> 00:18:16,340
to the crisis. The doomsday clock rewound again in 1963, 12 minutes to midnight. But

200
00:18:16,341 --> 00:18:30,341
the optimism was short-lived. In October 1964, China detonated an atomic bomb.

201
00:18:32,640 --> 00:18:39,160
China became the fifth nation to have a nuclear bomb, joining the United States, Russia, Great Britain and France.

202
00:18:39,161 --> 00:18:42,360
And the fear was it would now spiral out of control.

203
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:47,140
China actually was a country that advocated proliferation at that time.

204
00:18:49,580 --> 00:18:58,380
A global arms buildup and increased nationalism ultimately led to war.

205
00:18:58,600 --> 00:19:00,840
The U.S. and Vietnam in 1964.

206
00:19:02,640 --> 00:19:05,680
India and Pakistan in 1965.

207
00:19:07,180 --> 00:19:11,360
Israel against Jordan, Syria and Egypt in 1967.

208
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:21,560
By 1968, Eugene Rabinowitz believed that international cooperation was quickly being diminished by international anarchy.

209
00:19:22,120 --> 00:19:30,820
He wrote an apocryphal editorial under the title The Dismal Record and jumped the doomsday clock forward seven minutes to midnight.

210
00:19:32,640 --> 00:19:41,220
If the editorial mood at the Bulletin was gloomy, the scientists personally still held hope for the world.

211
00:19:41,640 --> 00:19:47,120
Kennedy started the effort, Johnson finished it, and Nixon signed it.

212
00:19:47,400 --> 00:19:49,860
And that was the Nuclear Non-Proliferation Treaty.

213
00:19:52,500 --> 00:19:59,980
The 1969 Treaty authorized the five nations holding nuclear weapons to help other states develop nuclear power.

214
00:19:59,981 --> 00:20:05,340
In turn, the non-nuclear states agreed not to develop or obtain nuclear weapons.

215
00:20:05,700 --> 00:20:11,860
It grew from an original 70 countries now to every country in the world except for India, Israel and Pakistan.

216
00:20:12,680 --> 00:20:15,000
Rabinowitz was encouraged by the agreement.

217
00:20:15,660 --> 00:20:20,960
In the April 1969 issue, he reset the clock's minute hand to 10 minutes.

218
00:20:26,160 --> 00:20:29,740
But hope waned with a new threat of nuclear war.

219
00:20:29,980 --> 00:20:34,840
It would be Europe that would be turned into cinders, not the United States or the Soviet Union.

220
00:20:39,100 --> 00:20:44,940
The Soviet Union Tsar Bama was the largest nuclear weapon ever constructed and detonated.

221
00:20:45,640 --> 00:20:53,860
Tested in 1961, the fusion bomb's yield was 50 megatons, with a design capable of an estimated 100 megatons.

222
00:20:54,540 --> 00:20:56,880
The Doomsday Clock will return in a moment.

223
00:20:59,980 --> 00:21:06,595
By the late 1960s, the arms race between the U.S.

224
00:21:06,596 --> 00:21:14,020
and the Soviet Union threatened to spin out of control.

225
00:21:18,660 --> 00:21:24,960
Finally, in 1969, the two superpowers agreed to discuss a reduction of nuclear weapons.

226
00:21:24,961 --> 00:21:30,980
The leaderships of the two countries had just encountered each other every once in a while at summit meetings,

227
00:21:31,120 --> 00:21:37,360
but they'd never really been able to engage on reducing or eliminating or controlling nuclear weapons.

228
00:21:37,960 --> 00:21:44,020
And in the late 1960s, the Soviets finally agreed that this is something that they would be willing at least to discuss.

229
00:21:44,720 --> 00:21:49,860
Three years after negotiations had begun at the Strategic Arms Limitation Talks, or SALT,

230
00:21:49,861 --> 00:21:55,400
President Richard Nixon and General Secretary Leonid Brezhnev signed two agreements in Moscow.

231
00:21:55,980 --> 00:21:59,920
The Anti-Ballistic Missile Treaty limited the deployment of ABM defenses,

232
00:22:00,400 --> 00:22:07,660
and the interim agreement froze the number of strategic ballistic missiles at 1972 levels, but only for five years.

233
00:22:11,240 --> 00:22:15,380
It was putting a cap on the arms race, essentially, and it worked.

234
00:22:15,381 --> 00:22:21,860
It didn't prevent the development of all nuclear weapons, but it prevented the arms race from spiraling completely out of control.

235
00:22:22,560 --> 00:22:27,820
In June 1972, Bernard Feld, a member of the Bulletin's Board of Directors,

236
00:22:28,220 --> 00:22:33,680
wrote that the board was generally pleased with the ABM treaty, but wary of the interim agreement.

237
00:22:34,540 --> 00:22:39,220
Still, SALT II treaty negotiations were set to begin later in the year.

238
00:22:39,740 --> 00:22:42,660
In a hopeful spirit, the clock was moved backwards.

239
00:22:42,661 --> 00:22:49,660
The new time, 12 minutes to midnight, then the farthest jump back.

240
00:22:53,660 --> 00:22:57,470
On May 15, 1973, Eugene Rabinowich died.

241
00:22:57,471 --> 00:23:03,660
The bulletin lost its principal intellectual and editorial voice.

242
00:23:03,661 --> 00:23:09,660
Fifty-three-year-old physicist Bernard Feld became the new editor-in-chief.

243
00:23:09,661 --> 00:23:13,660
As a research scientist, he had helped usher in the atomic era.

244
00:23:13,661 --> 00:23:18,660
As a concerned scientist, he advocated nuclear disarmament.

245
00:23:18,661 --> 00:23:24,660
By 1974, the Bulletin's board adopted a new system to reset the doomsday clock.

246
00:23:24,661 --> 00:23:27,660
It would no longer be the decision of one man.

247
00:23:27,661 --> 00:23:31,660
Instead, members would have to come to a consensus.

248
00:23:31,661 --> 00:23:35,260
In May, the board made its first decision to reset the clock.

249
00:23:39,260 --> 00:23:42,660
India exploded what it called a nuclear device.

250
00:23:42,960 --> 00:23:46,840
They didn't call it a bomb. They were very adamant about not calling it a bomb,

251
00:23:46,940 --> 00:23:49,180
but everybody knew exactly what it was.

252
00:23:50,860 --> 00:23:55,120
In 1974, the new time was nine minutes before midnight.

253
00:23:55,121 --> 00:23:56,600
It wouldn't stop there.

254
00:23:56,601 --> 00:24:09,180
Five years later, on December 26, 1979, the Soviet Union dispatched tanks and troops to Afghanistan to prop up a pro-Soviet government.

255
00:24:09,680 --> 00:24:12,580
The relationship between Moscow and Washington worsened.

256
00:24:12,581 --> 00:24:22,674
There was great fear that this was an early sign that the Soviet Union was going to be throwing its military weight around and might even decide it was time to invade

257
00:24:22,675 --> 00:24:23,375
Europe.

258
00:24:23,376 --> 00:24:28,300
So it was of great concern to the NATO alliance and to the United States as well.

259
00:24:28,620 --> 00:24:32,400
President Jimmy Carter asked the Senate to postpone action on SALT II,

260
00:24:32,540 --> 00:24:37,340
the arms limitation initiative that he and General Secretary Brezhnev had recently signed.

261
00:24:37,780 --> 00:24:42,520
Afghanistan just completely pushed the U.S.-Soviet relationship off track.

262
00:24:44,160 --> 00:24:51,320
Bernard Feld offered his general assessment of an unstable world in the bulletin's January 1980 issue.

263
00:24:51,321 --> 00:24:53,960
SALT II had stalled again.

264
00:24:54,920 --> 00:24:57,680
Religious fanaticism in the Middle East was on the rise,

265
00:24:58,280 --> 00:25:01,540
and millions were being killed by the Khmer Rouge in Cambodia.

266
00:25:04,220 --> 00:25:08,440
The minute hand was moved forward and set at 7 minutes to midnight.

267
00:25:09,300 --> 00:25:13,140
Here we are, back again at 7 minutes to midnight, back where we started.

268
00:25:13,500 --> 00:25:16,640
This was a very troubling time for a lot of the people at the Bulletin,

269
00:25:16,800 --> 00:25:18,940
and I think they wanted to reflect that on the clock.

270
00:25:18,941 --> 00:25:25,100
The new decade had barely begun when suddenly nuclear war in Europe seemed possible.

271
00:25:25,340 --> 00:25:28,980
The Soviet Union had deployed a new generation of strategic weapons.

272
00:25:30,200 --> 00:25:36,000
They were called the SS-20 in related systems, and these were intermediate range weapons.

273
00:25:36,140 --> 00:25:42,060
That is, from the territory of the Soviet Union, they could strike all the major capitals of Europe.

274
00:25:43,060 --> 00:25:44,699
As a countermeasure, the U.S.

275
00:25:44,700 --> 00:25:48,260
deployed its own missiles on hair-trigger alert in NATO Europe.

276
00:25:48,940 --> 00:25:53,580
We called ours the Pershing missiles and the ground-launched cruise missiles.

277
00:25:53,940 --> 00:25:57,480
So a new phase of the nuclear arms race opened up in Europe.

278
00:25:58,360 --> 00:26:00,580
That if there was a conflict and it went nuclear,

279
00:26:01,020 --> 00:26:03,600
it would be Europe that would be turned into cinders,

280
00:26:03,880 --> 00:26:05,940
not the United States or the Soviet Union.

281
00:26:08,860 --> 00:26:12,000
January 1981, Bernard Feld wrote,

282
00:26:12,400 --> 00:26:17,900
Both sides willfully delude themselves that a nuclear war can remain limited or even be won.

283
00:26:17,901 --> 00:26:22,160
In 1980, both sides officially declared nuclear war thinkable.

284
00:26:25,360 --> 00:26:29,800
Thinkable dictated a radical change, four minutes to midnight.

285
00:26:31,440 --> 00:26:34,000
The President of the United States.

286
00:26:34,720 --> 00:26:41,480
The weapons buildup that began with President Carter was expanded and accelerated under President Ronald Reagan.

287
00:26:41,481 --> 00:26:49,020
He had campaigned on the position that the United States had become dangerously weak in relation to the Soviet Union.

288
00:26:57,600 --> 00:27:00,820
SALT II was, in his opinion, fatally flawed.

289
00:27:01,140 --> 00:27:05,940
The way to end the Cold War was to win it by destroying the evil Soviet Union.

290
00:27:05,941 --> 00:27:14,520
Let us be aware that while they preach the supremacy of the state, they are the focus of evil in the modern world.

291
00:27:15,900 --> 00:27:18,700
The bulletin had no illusions about the Soviet Union.

292
00:27:19,180 --> 00:27:22,140
They simply thought the two countries had to work together.

293
00:27:22,760 --> 00:27:29,340
When Reagan made his evil empire speech, it seemed to indicate to many, including editors at the bulletin,

294
00:27:30,080 --> 00:27:33,380
that Reagan had a different vision, and that scared them greatly.

295
00:27:33,381 --> 00:27:38,380
Reagan purported that America needed to construct an impermeable shield in space.

296
00:27:38,381 --> 00:27:44,380
In 1983, he announced an extensive research and development effort, the Strategic Defense Initiative,

297
00:27:44,381 --> 00:27:48,380
for the purpose of building the perfect missile defense program.

298
00:27:48,381 --> 00:27:53,380
We seek neither military superiority nor political advantage.

299
00:27:53,381 --> 00:28:01,380
Our only purpose, one all people share, is to search for ways to reduce the danger of nuclear war.

300
00:28:01,800 --> 00:28:05,780
Critics quickly dubbed Reagan's plan Star Wars after the movie.

301
00:28:05,781 --> 00:28:10,640
They complained that if it worked, it would violate the anti-ballistic missile treaty,

302
00:28:10,980 --> 00:28:13,140
encourage the militarization of space,

303
00:28:13,560 --> 00:28:17,780
and lead to the resumption of an all-out nuclear arms race.

304
00:28:18,180 --> 00:28:22,900
As soon as the United States contemplated a perfect ballistic missile defense,

305
00:28:23,660 --> 00:28:28,860
then the Russian strategic arsenal and its capabilities was undermined.

306
00:28:28,861 --> 00:28:34,860
And so as a result, the Soviets were initially very, very concerned about the strategic defense initiative.

307
00:28:36,460 --> 00:28:40,360
January 1984. Bulletin editors reacted.

308
00:28:40,960 --> 00:28:44,900
The doomsday clock moved dramatically forward, three minutes to midnight.

309
00:28:45,320 --> 00:28:51,580
The closest it had come to zero hour since the Soviets' detonation of the hydrogen bomb in 1953.

310
00:28:51,581 --> 00:29:00,260
I think it was right for many people, both professional people, such as the people employed at the bulletin,

311
00:29:00,300 --> 00:29:05,280
as well as just everyday folks to be very, very concerned about where things were going to go next.

312
00:29:05,460 --> 00:29:06,900
And that's what the clock was reflecting.

313
00:29:09,000 --> 00:29:14,300
But then the two superpowers did something that struck the editors of the bulletin as near miraculous.

314
00:29:14,880 --> 00:29:16,980
This was simply unheard of historically.

315
00:29:21,580 --> 00:29:29,400
A recent study estimates that more than 28,000 nuclear weapons are currently maintained by

316
00:29:29,401 --> 00:29:33,920
the world's eight known nuclear powers, most in the U.S. and Russia.

317
00:29:33,921 --> 00:29:40,240
The Doomsday Clock will return in a moment.

318
00:29:40,241 --> 00:29:44,220
We maintain our strength in order to deter and defend against aggression.

319
00:29:44,221 --> 00:29:49,080
While President Reagan's strategic defense initiative threatened U.S.-Soviet relations,

320
00:29:49,081 --> 00:29:52,820
The Bulletin's board of directors found a reason for optimism.

321
00:29:52,821 --> 00:29:57,680
The Soviet Union and the United States had agreed on a treaty that eliminated a whole

322
00:29:57,681 --> 00:30:00,360
class of weapons.

323
00:30:00,361 --> 00:30:05,260
After years of deadlock, President Ronald Reagan and General Secretary Mikhail Gorbachev

324
00:30:05,261 --> 00:30:09,000
had negotiated an arms agreement in 1987.

325
00:30:09,001 --> 00:30:14,060
The Intermediate Range Nuclear Forces Treaty not only eliminated an entire class of weapons

326
00:30:14,061 --> 00:30:18,600
in Europe, but from all U.S. and Soviet arsenals.

327
00:30:18,601 --> 00:30:20,500
was simply unheard of historically.

328
00:30:21,160 --> 00:30:23,860
And it showed, I think, that the two countries were beginning,

329
00:30:24,320 --> 00:30:26,780
even then, the process of opening

330
00:30:26,781 --> 00:30:30,640
that led eventually to the demise of the Soviet Union.

331
00:30:30,641 --> 00:30:32,080
It was a simple proposal.

332
00:30:32,280 --> 00:30:34,460
One might say disarmingly simple.

333
00:30:35,380 --> 00:30:38,080
The INF Treaty struck many,

334
00:30:38,400 --> 00:30:41,520
including the bulletin's board members, as remarkable.

335
00:30:42,760 --> 00:30:46,100
The Reagan administration, by the end of its first term,

336
00:30:46,140 --> 00:30:46,960
was on the defensive.

337
00:30:46,961 --> 00:30:58,500
It had come in with very strong pro-nuclear weapons policies and was met by a global wave of protests against the policies.

338
00:31:00,840 --> 00:31:12,080
It wasn't until the second term that Reagan really turned from a defensive buildup to an arms negotiated reduction process.

339
00:31:12,081 --> 00:31:18,306
And in the second term, Ronald Reagan became probably the greatest arms control president in U.S.

340
00:31:18,307 --> 00:31:19,007
history.

341
00:31:19,008 --> 00:31:25,440
For the first time in history, the language of arms control was replaced by arms reduction.

342
00:31:27,100 --> 00:31:29,740
The events had an impact on the bulletin.

343
00:31:30,280 --> 00:31:34,980
In 1988, the clock was reset to six minutes before midnight.

344
00:31:35,780 --> 00:31:40,500
The board members had always tried to spot trends before they reset the clock,

345
00:31:40,501 --> 00:31:43,540
but they could not have imagined what occurred next.

346
00:31:47,220 --> 00:31:51,300
On November 9th, 1989, the Berlin Wall fell,

347
00:31:51,680 --> 00:31:55,060
a resounding signal that the Cold War was near an end.

348
00:31:55,380 --> 00:31:59,380
Prediction changed a lot after 1989 and the fall of the Berlin Wall

349
00:31:59,381 --> 00:32:03,320
because so many things happened that many of us thought we wouldn't see in our lifetimes.

350
00:32:10,500 --> 00:32:16,500
Popular democratic revolution swept every communist regime in Eastern Europe out of power.

351
00:32:16,501 --> 00:32:20,000
The doomsday clock received an overdue facelift.

352
00:32:22,000 --> 00:32:24,800
Bulletin needed to change because the world was changing.

353
00:32:25,500 --> 00:32:27,160
And so the clock was redesigned.

354
00:32:27,440 --> 00:32:33,760
And Martell created a new version of the clock that had the hands centered over the earth.

355
00:32:34,320 --> 00:32:39,860
And that was then translated into a metal version of the clock that we continue to use today.

356
00:32:40,500 --> 00:32:50,980
April 1990, the new clock marked the end of the Cold War with a new time, 10 minutes to

357
00:32:50,981 --> 00:32:54,300
midnight.

358
00:32:54,301 --> 00:32:59,380
Although his popularity at home was waning, Gorbachev was still able to broker the Strategic

359
00:32:59,381 --> 00:33:05,260
Arms Reduction Treaty, or START, in 1991, before his exit from power.

360
00:33:05,261 --> 00:33:13,780
not only did it cut the arsenals of the united states and soviet union by half

361
00:33:13,781 --> 00:33:20,020
each country having 12 000 warheads it cut the numbers down to 6 000 on each side

362
00:33:20,021 --> 00:33:29,800
but it also put in place a major very intrusive inspection regime 17 minutes to midnight the move

363
00:33:29,801 --> 00:33:32,640
was literally off the scale.

364
00:33:32,641 --> 00:33:35,340
17 minutes to midnight, the furthest that it's ever gone.

365
00:33:35,341 --> 00:33:37,960
And the board and the editors were very clear

366
00:33:37,961 --> 00:33:39,560
about why that was done, because the world

367
00:33:39,561 --> 00:33:42,520
had changed dramatically, and all of the old paradigms

368
00:33:42,521 --> 00:33:43,221
were gone.

369
00:33:43,222 --> 00:33:45,280
One of the board members, John Simpson,

370
00:33:45,281 --> 00:33:47,280
who had been around at the curation,

371
00:33:47,281 --> 00:33:50,380
said 15 minutes was all we would ever need.

372
00:33:50,381 --> 00:33:53,040
And by God, we moved it off the scale

373
00:33:53,041 --> 00:33:54,640
to show how optimistic we were.

374
00:33:59,800 --> 00:34:11,008
As the bulletin's 50th anniversary approached in 1995, it was felt that experts in foreign policy and weapons technology could help board members see the world with

375
00:34:11,009 --> 00:34:11,760
fresh eyes.

376
00:34:11,880 --> 00:34:17,540
On December 7th, a public hearing in Chicago invited new opinion about the clock.

377
00:34:17,760 --> 00:34:24,200
One man I remember in particular was Henry Beanan, and he said, well, maybe there shouldn't be any clock at all.

378
00:34:24,900 --> 00:34:28,440
His position was that Russia and the United States were no longer enemies,

379
00:34:29,040 --> 00:34:31,340
and there was no real need for the clock.

380
00:34:31,580 --> 00:34:35,840
Another expert thought the clock should be moved back even further, to 20 minutes,

381
00:34:35,841 --> 00:34:39,680
because she believed the meaning of Doomsday had changed.

382
00:34:40,120 --> 00:34:43,880
Doomsday was not so clearly defined as the end of civilization.

383
00:34:44,100 --> 00:34:47,120
Doomsday now met the use of nuclear weapons anywhere in the world.

384
00:34:47,800 --> 00:34:51,360
So metaphorically speaking, there's life after midnight for most of the world,

385
00:34:51,361 --> 00:34:56,080
but not for the people who were involved in whatever local nuclear war there was.

386
00:34:56,720 --> 00:35:02,040
After a day of debate, board members spent 90 minutes deciding how much to move the clock.

387
00:35:02,380 --> 00:35:06,760
They put the minute hand back on the scale at 14 minutes to doomsday.

388
00:35:11,120 --> 00:35:17,820
With the decline of the Soviet Union and basically their poverty,

389
00:35:17,821 --> 00:35:23,340
There was a lot of worry that small tactical nukes would be traded on the black market.

390
00:35:23,640 --> 00:35:28,880
There was also a great deal of fear that Russian scientists, who were basically out of a job,

391
00:35:29,400 --> 00:35:34,260
would sell their services to countries that were interested in building weapons.

392
00:35:36,240 --> 00:35:40,640
Three years later, board members noticed increasing threats to world peace.

393
00:35:41,060 --> 00:35:45,960
In 1998, India and Pakistan both went nuclear officially.

394
00:35:46,760 --> 00:35:50,120
India conducted tests, followed by Pakistan.

395
00:35:50,920 --> 00:35:52,680
It was a very scary time.

396
00:35:52,800 --> 00:35:56,420
These are major countries with enormous populations

397
00:35:56,421 --> 00:36:01,000
who have already had three wars with each other since 1945.

398
00:36:02,520 --> 00:36:10,800
And it did look like a very major step toward proliferation and possibly war.

399
00:36:10,801 --> 00:36:12,760
Board members acted quickly.

400
00:36:12,761 --> 00:36:20,080
In June 1998, one month after the explosions, the clock was reset, nine minutes to midnight.

401
00:36:20,720 --> 00:36:26,060
Only once before, in 1968, had the hand moved forward so many minutes.

402
00:36:42,760 --> 00:36:50,840
9-11, there was talk of retaliation against the Taliban and Osama bin Laden,

403
00:36:50,841 --> 00:36:57,600
of taking the war against terrorism to Afghanistan, and of suitcase-sized nuclear bombs.

404
00:36:58,040 --> 00:37:01,320
Yet the doomsday clock was not reset immediately.

405
00:37:02,060 --> 00:37:02,760
Why?

406
00:37:02,761 --> 00:37:12,880
Since 1945, there have been an estimated 2,045 nuclear weapons tests worldwide.

407
00:37:13,540 --> 00:37:17,820
That's the equivalent of one test every 10 days for the last 60 years.

408
00:37:18,420 --> 00:37:20,800
The Doomsday Clock will return in a moment.

409
00:37:24,800 --> 00:37:26,160
Oh my goodness!

410
00:37:26,161 --> 00:37:32,540
September 11, 2001.

411
00:37:33,040 --> 00:37:37,680
Hijacked jetliners hit the twin towers of the World Trade Center in New York and the

412
00:37:37,681 --> 00:37:39,740
Pentagon outside Washington, D.C.

413
00:37:39,741 --> 00:37:43,520
A fourth hijacked plane crashed into a field in Pennsylvania.

414
00:37:56,160 --> 00:38:07,840
Trading on Wall Street stopped.

415
00:38:07,841 --> 00:38:13,280
The Federal Aviation Administration halted all flight operations at the nation's airports

416
00:38:13,281 --> 00:38:15,800
for the first time in U.S. history.

417
00:38:16,680 --> 00:38:19,800
The U.S. military was placed on high alert.

418
00:38:20,320 --> 00:38:23,460
Hundreds of New York City firefighters and police officers

419
00:38:23,461 --> 00:38:28,040
sent to rescue World Trade Center workers died when the towers collapsed.

420
00:38:36,820 --> 00:38:40,200
In all, nearly 2,800 people died.

421
00:38:43,980 --> 00:38:47,220
But the doomsday clock did not budge.

422
00:38:53,460 --> 00:39:00,700
Many people, judging from the emails and phone calls that we got, were very concerned and wanted to know if the clock would move.

423
00:39:00,820 --> 00:39:06,080
And we had to explain, as I'm explaining now, that the board wanted to take some time and see where things were going.

424
00:39:06,520 --> 00:39:09,780
The attacks themselves, of course, were very significant, but what would happen as a result?

425
00:39:09,900 --> 00:39:12,550
How would policy change? Would there be a war?

426
00:39:12,551 --> 00:39:14,740
Were there going to be future attacks?

427
00:39:14,820 --> 00:39:17,380
These were all questions that were raised.

428
00:39:17,381 --> 00:39:30,769
We don't tend to respond instantaneously to events, and the extent to which they represented a true, long-standing change in global security certainly wasn't clear

429
00:39:30,770 --> 00:39:31,470
then.

430
00:39:32,300 --> 00:39:41,420
In the months following September 11th, an increased risk of nuclear terrorism by al-Qaeda and other terrorist groups filled the news.

431
00:39:42,120 --> 00:39:49,940
Finally, in February 2002, the clock moved closer to midnight by two minutes, seven minutes to doomsday.

432
00:39:56,320 --> 00:40:00,500
The 2001 war in Afghanistan was an important reason.

433
00:40:00,501 --> 00:40:05,460
That, in fact, did factor into our decision to move the clock back in 2002,

434
00:40:05,760 --> 00:40:09,820
to the original time at which the bulletin clock was set, the very first time.

435
00:40:09,821 --> 00:40:14,620
What will it take to move the clock through the world's next crisis?

436
00:40:15,140 --> 00:40:16,460
And in which direction?

437
00:40:16,880 --> 00:40:19,460
I have no reason to believe that the clock will move anytime soon,

438
00:40:19,560 --> 00:40:22,040
but on the other hand, I have no reason to believe that it won't.

439
00:40:22,760 --> 00:40:25,040
The board talks frequently.

440
00:40:25,380 --> 00:40:29,280
We're looking at the balance of nuclear power among the superpowers.

441
00:40:29,420 --> 00:40:31,200
We're looking at nuclear weapons development.

442
00:40:31,201 --> 00:40:34,720
And we're also looking at a range of broader security issues

443
00:40:34,721 --> 00:40:39,520
having to do with conventional, non-nuclear weapons and stability and security.

444
00:40:39,820 --> 00:40:44,820
We don't issue votes, we don't talk about who said what, and that's quite deliberate

445
00:40:44,821 --> 00:40:47,820
because this is a decision that's arrived at by a group of people.

446
00:40:47,821 --> 00:40:52,820
Board members see the job as history-making and take it very seriously.

447
00:40:52,821 --> 00:40:58,820
What happened, and it really happened inadvertently, is that the clock became part of history.

448
00:40:58,821 --> 00:41:02,820
And so the responsibility that's attached to that is huge.

449
00:41:02,821 --> 00:41:07,820
These are some of the most serious discussions I ever engage in, the discussions about the clock.

450
00:41:09,820 --> 00:41:12,580
I got an email from somebody saying,

451
00:41:12,780 --> 00:41:15,460
couldn't we make the world safer by moving the minute hand back?

452
00:41:15,960 --> 00:41:19,220
As if the clock itself was controlling the fate of the world.

453
00:41:19,420 --> 00:41:21,520
And I wrote back and I thought I wish that were the case.

454
00:41:22,340 --> 00:41:25,620
But unfortunately, the clock responds to world events.

455
00:41:25,720 --> 00:41:26,700
It doesn't control them.

456
00:41:28,100 --> 00:41:32,500
In the post-9-11 era, the clock's ticking sounds a new urgency.

457
00:41:32,501 --> 00:41:36,420
This is an extremely critical time in history.

458
00:41:36,421 --> 00:41:43,420
We are climbing down from the first great wave of proliferation that began after World War II.

459
00:41:43,421 --> 00:41:53,420
The bad news is that there's a group of countries, friends and foes, that are now interested in acquiring nuclear weapons.

460
00:41:53,421 --> 00:41:54,465
weapons.

461
00:41:54,466 --> 00:42:04,920
The clock, more than any other symbol I can think of, is a public warning system

462
00:42:04,921 --> 00:42:07,864
to say, look, something serious is happening here.

463
00:42:07,865 --> 00:42:10,220
Stop what you're doing just for a minute

464
00:42:10,221 --> 00:42:12,100
and pay attention to this.

465
00:42:12,101 --> 00:42:16,440
The superpowers have stepped away from their launch buttons,

466
00:42:16,441 --> 00:42:21,720
but threats from North Korea, continuing hostilities between India and Pakistan,

467
00:42:21,721 --> 00:42:25,460
and pressure by terrorists keep the world on edge.

468
00:42:38,400 --> 00:42:42,040
The nuclear age is just over half a century old,

469
00:42:42,280 --> 00:42:45,360
hardly a grain of sand in the hourglass of civilization.

470
00:42:46,140 --> 00:42:50,100
The future of the doomsday clock depends less on the hands of time

471
00:42:50,101 --> 00:42:54,000
than on what deadly weapons fall into the hands of people.

472
00:42:55,940 --> 00:43:02,380
The clock, it's a simple reminder that while we're talking,

473
00:43:03,360 --> 00:43:06,060
those 30,000 nuclear weapons could be used at any time,

474
00:43:06,840 --> 00:43:12,360
that half of them are on constant alert, and that this is a constant danger.

475
00:43:12,660 --> 00:43:16,100
One side or the other could initiate the use of some of those,

476
00:43:16,101 --> 00:43:19,740
and the other side would almost certainly respond with nuclear weapons.

477
00:43:19,741 --> 00:43:20,580
and where would it end?

478
00:43:20,581 --> 00:43:23,360
Nobody has ever been able to convince me

479
00:43:23,361 --> 00:43:25,300
that they knew how to stop a nuclear war

480
00:43:25,301 --> 00:43:26,220
once it started.

